  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 212 PM MDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nThe long term will be largely uneventful as we see high pressure\nbuilding back in, bringing warm and dry weather alongside a \nrisk of critical fire weather conditions. Wednesday starts with \nsome lingering precipitation in the Nebraska Panhandle as the \ntrough is departing the region, but this will be quickly \nescaping and bringing in a dry spell that will last through the \nremainder of the long term. That being said, Wednesday should \nstill be quite a fair late summer day, with temperatures in the \n70's to 80's across our area thanks to the cold front that's \ncurrently passing over the region. But a high pressure ridge \nwill build back in, dominating into the weekend with highs \ngradually rising back into the 90's to triple digits for most of\nour CWA outside of the mountains. Along with this will come dry\nair, with daytime relative humidity values into the single \ndigit to teens each day. Because of this, expect an elevated \nfire weather environment, with the potential to become critical \nwith any increase in winds. This could be notably possible over \nthe weekend when the ridge flattens due to troughing to our \nnorth, causing an enhanced pressure gradient primarily for our \nnorthern zones bringing breezy winds. Otherwise, summer has no \nintention of leaving early as our streak of warm temperatures \ncontinues.",
